Now let me look at the key part: the `rejectWebchatSessionMutation` function in `sessions.ts`:
```typescript
function rejectWebchatSessionMutation(params: {
  action: "patch" | "delete";
  client: GatewayClient | null;
  isWebchatConnect: (params: GatewayClient["connect"] | null | undefined) => boolean;
  respond: RespondFn;
}): boolean {
  if (!params.client?.connect || !params.isWebchatConnect(params.client.connect)) {
    return false;  // Not a webchat client, allow
  }
  if (params.client.connect.client.id === GATEWAY_CLIENT_IDS.CONTROL_UI) {
    return false;  // Control UI is allowed
  }
  // Other webchat clients are rejected
  params.respond(
    false,
    undefined,
    errorShape(
      ErrorCodes.INVALID_REQUEST,
      `webchat clients cannot ${params.action} sessions; use chat.send for session-scoped updates`,
    ),
  );
  return true;
}
```

So Control UI clients are explicitly allowed to patch and delete sessions, but other webchat clients get rejected with a suggestion to use `chat.send` instead. The function checks if the client is connected and is a webchat connection, then either permits the operation or blocks it with an error response.

Now I need to understand what `isWebchatConnect` actually validates—whether it identifies any client in webchat mode or specifically the webchat-ui client ID.

