The test expects `sessions.patch` to be called with `model: "openai/gpt-5-mini"`. This is the FULL format with provider prefix.


But before this fix, the dropdown values were just the model ID like `"gpt-5.2"`, which the server would resolve using the session's current provider—potentially the wrong one. The fix changes the dropdown to use `"provider/model"` format so the correct provider gets sent. This was the actual bug.

However, even with the fix in place, there's still the question of whether the server properly persists and retrieves the model override when it's sent. Looking at the issue title again—"Control UI session model override dropdown not working (webchat)"—I'm wondering if the dropdown might be connected to a different issue entirely, like how the webchat client itself works rather than just the session patch logic.

Let me refocus on what's actually happening: the model selector displays the selected model, but when a message gets sent, the gateway uses the default instead of the override. The `sessions.patch` call might succeed and update the local cache, but then `refreshSessionOptions` reloads the session from the server and overwrites that optimistic update. Or perhaps the session key being used isn't the right one, so the override gets stored under the wrong key.

Looking at `refreshSessionOptions`, it calls `loadSessions` which should pull fresh session data from the gateway. If the model override was actually persisted server-side, it should come back in that response. But if the override was cleared due to validation failure, it would show the default instead. So the real question is whether `sessions.patch` is actually persisting the override correctly in the first place.

I'm going to move forward and write up my findings. The issue appears to stem from a mismatch between what the user selects in the dropdown and what gets persisted—if the model ID alone is selected without the provider prefix, the server might resolve it differently than intended, or if the provider itself doesn't match the session's current provider, the validation could fail silently.

Looking at the fix that was implemented, the real problem was that the dropdown was sending bare model IDs like `"gpt-5.2"` instead of the fully qualified `"openai/gpt-5.2"`. The backend would then fall back to using the session's existing provider (like "anthropic"), creating a mismatch like `"anthropic/gpt-5.2"` instead of the intended `"openai/gpt-5.2"`. The solution ensures the dropdown always sends the complete provider-prefixed model identifier.

